# 09_global_executable — decision: no Python port

Reviewed 2026-07-24. Decided (with the user) not to create
`week1_baseline/python/09_global_executable/`.

## Why

Every step 00-08 is a literal architectural mirror: same classes, same
method names, same behavior, ported line-by-line from Ruby. `09` breaks
that premise — its entire content is RubyGems packaging mechanics
(`boukensha.gemspec`, `bin/boukensha` shebang stub, `lib/boukensha_loader.rb`
resolving `BOUKENSHA_PATH` / `~/.boukensharc` / a bundled default), not
agent-loop code. There is no `lib/boukensha/*.rb` logic change in this step
beyond the routine regression set (see below) — the actual new surface
area is "how RubyGems installs a global executable," which has no Ruby
source to mirror on the Python side. A Python equivalent (`pyproject.toml`
entry points + `uv tool install`) would mean inventing new Python-specific
packaging config from scratch, not translating this step's file — a
different kind of exercise than every prior port, and not really "porting
09" so much as "building a new, unrelated packaging story."

## What was still done for 09 on the Ruby side

Reviewed the step, then live-ran `bin/boukensha` end-to-end (piped stdin
task + `/exit`, `BOUKENSHA_DIR` pointed at the repo-root `.boukensha/`)
and fixed the regressions it surfaced — same standing set as every prior
step, reapplied here:

- `context.rb` — `register_tool` missing `.to_s` normalization (10th
  occurrence).
- `agent.rb` — tool-dispatch logging `begin/rescue/else` scope regressed
  to a single `begin/rescue` (4th occurrence).
- `backends/anthropic.rb` — `claude-sonnet-5` MODELS entry missing
  (confirmed live: this is what actually crashed the first test run,
  `UnsupportedModelError`); `to_messages(system, messages)` arity
  regressed to single-arg, also fixed in `backends/gemini.rb` and
  `prompt_builder.rb`.
- `config.rb` — `PROMPTS_DIR` hop count regressed from `../../prompts` to
  `../../../prompts` (one hop too many — inverse of the usual too-few-hops
  bug); `resolve_dir`'s 3-tier fallback (env var → cwd `.boukensha/` →
  home default, added in `08`) had collapsed back to a 2-tier
  `ENV.fetch(...) || DEFAULT_DIR`, losing the cwd tier entirely.
- `client.rb` — the friendly 401 "authentication failed — check your API
  key" message (added in `08`) was gone.
- `repl.rb` — the banner's `✓/✗ API key set` and `✗ directory not found`
  checks (added in `08`) had regressed to a plainer 3-line dump; the
  orphaned-message rollback fix (`rollback_point` + `slice!` in both
  rescue clauses, the real bug found during `08`'s port) was gone
  entirely.

After fixes, `bin/boukensha` runs cleanly end-to-end: banner renders with
the richer status checks, resolves `settings.yaml`/`.env` from
`BOUKENSHA_DIR`, and completes a real turn against the Anthropic API.

## If a Python port of "global executable" is wanted later

Would need to be scoped as a new design question, not a mechanical port:
what selects "which step" in Python (there's no single bundled default
package the way the gem has one lib/ tree), whether `uv tool install`,
`pipx`, or a plain `pyproject.toml` `[project.scripts]` entry point is the
right mechanism, and whether `BOUKENSHA_PATH`/`~/.boukensharc`-style step
switching is even a goal for the Python side. Not started.
